const figmaUrl = 'https://www.figma.com/proto/edpFxVc5O79LOaxaQ0CVYD/Portfolio-example?page-id=290%3A2&node-id=628-354&viewport=654%2C385%2C0.22&t=OjeEBw6ffcriMrTZ-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=628%3A354';
---
<BaseLayout title="Medical claim redesign — Dana" description="A guided, responsive, and accessible medical claim experience for Canada Life plan members.">
  <header class="case-hero"><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Canada Life · Released product</p><h1>Redesigning the post-visit medical claim experience</h1><p class="lead">A guided, responsive, and accessible reimbursement flow for plan members submitting medical expenses and supporting documents.</p></div></header>

  <section class="snapshot"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project snapshot</p><h2>Modernizing a high-use benefits journey</h2><p>The experience was redesigned during a broader migration from SAP Hybris to Salesforce Industries. It had to simplify claim submission while supporting complex benefit rules, responsive layouts, technical constraints, and accessibility requirements.</p></div><dl><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>UX Designer, end-to-end product experience</dd></div><div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Group-benefits members requesting reimbursement after receiving care</dd></div><div><dt>Platforms</dt><dd>Responsive web and mobile</dd></div><div><dt>Partners</dt><dd>Product, research, content, accessibility, QA, and engineering</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Released within a platform serving 10M+ users</dd></div></dl></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Members needed clarity at every step</h2><p class="large">After paying for an eligible medical service, members needed to provide practitioner information, expense details, and supporting documents to request reimbursement. The original experience was lengthy, inconsistent, and difficult to use on mobile, leaving members uncertain about requirements and completion.</p><div class="challenge"><strong>Design challenge</strong><p>How might we create a clearer claim journey while supporting multiple claim types, platform migration, responsive behavior, and accessibility requirements?</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="legacy"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Previous experience · SAP Hybris</p><h2>The legacy journey exposed complexity at every stage</h2><p>These annotated wireframes document the experience before the platform migration. Members moved through a long sequence of claim-type decisions, eligibility questions, expense fields, document uploads, and final confirmation.</p></div><aside class="legacy-summary"><strong>What the review revealed</strong><p>The journey contained at least 12 annotated actions from sign-in to submission. Long forms and instructions competed for attention, while the desktop-first layout made an already demanding task harder to complete on smaller screens.</p></aside><div class="legacy-gallery"><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-01-entry.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-01-entry.png" alt="Legacy Hybris claim landing page with the Start online claim action annotated" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>01 · Entry point</b><span>Members began from a separate claim landing page.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-02-claim-type.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-02-claim-type.png" alt="Legacy Hybris page asking members to select a claim type" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>02 · Claim type</b><span>The flow branched early based on the expense category.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-03-questions.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-03-questions.png" alt="Legacy Hybris form containing a dense set of eligibility questions" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>03 · Eligibility questions</b><span>Several decisions appeared together in one dense form.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-04-expense-details.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-04-expense-details.png" alt="Legacy Hybris form for receipt and expense details" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>04 · Expense details</b><span>Receipt information required another detailed form.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-05-documents.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-05-documents.png" alt="Legacy Hybris document upload page with supporting information" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>05 · Supporting documents</b><span>Upload guidance and file actions shared the same screen.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-06-review.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/legacy/old-06-review.png" alt="Legacy Hybris confirmation page shown before claim submission" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>06 · Review and submit</b><span>Members reviewed the accumulated information before submitting.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p class="artifact-note">Select any wireframe to view it at full size. Screens use sample account data from the original portfolio artifact.</p></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container grid evidence"><div><p class="eyebrow">Research and validation</p><h2>Testing the structure before refining the interface</h2><p>The redesign used two rounds of usability testing with plan members. Wireframes were used to evaluate flow structure and comprehension; high-fidelity prototypes were used to examine readability, hierarchy, and accessibility.</p></div><div class="evidence-list"><article><b>Round 1</b><h3>Wireframe validation</h3><p>Tested whether the sequence and grouping of questions made sense before visual refinement.</p></article><article><b>Round 2</b><h3>High-fidelity validation</h3><p>Tested clarity, visual hierarchy, upload guidance, and the overall sense of confidence.</p></article><article><b>Refinements</b><h3>What changed</h3><p>Feedback informed content hierarchy, button language, and instructions for supporting documents.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="released-flow"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Released experience · Evidence review</p><h2>Seventy-nine screens, distilled into one coherent journey</h2><p>I reviewed the complete set of 79 unique desktop and mobile screenshots as a system—not as isolated pages. The evidence covered claims, estimates, responsive layouts, navigation states, document uploads, recovery guidance, and confirmation patterns. The six frames below show the core medical-claim story while keeping the adjacent estimate flow outside this case study’s main narrative.</p></div><div class="evidence-audit" aria-label="Screenshot evidence summary"><article><strong>79</strong><span>unique screens reviewed</span></article><article><strong>2</strong><span>responsive contexts: desktop and mobile</span></article><article><strong>6</strong><span>de-identified frames selected</span></article></div><ol class="released-gallery"><li><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/01-claim-entry.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/01-claim-entry.webp" alt="Released Canada Life Make a claim page with a prominent Claims made easy entry card" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>01 · Prepare</b><span>A focused entry card sets expectations before the member begins.</span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/02-basic-information.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/02-basic-information.webp" alt="Released basic information step with a progress indicator and person selection" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>02 · Establish context</b><span>Progressive disclosure starts with only the person and plan needed for the claim.</span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/03-claim-categories.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/03-claim-categories.webp" alt="Released claim type page presenting health expense categories as scannable cards" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>03 · Choose a category</b><span>Plain-language cards replace a dense list of claim types.</span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/04-recovery-guidance.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/04-recovery-guidance.webp" alt="Released recovery page helping members return to claim categories or continue with another claim type" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>04 · Recover with confidence</b><span>A clear decision point helps members who cannot find the right category.</span></figcaption></figure></li><li><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/05-document-upload.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/05-document-upload.webp" alt="Released document upload step with separate areas for a claim form and supporting receipts" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>05 · Provide evidence</b><span>Upload requirements, file limits, and terms appear at the point of need.</span></figcaption></figure></li><li class="mobile-frame"><figure><a href="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/06-mobile-entry.webp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/06-mobile-entry.webp" alt="Mobile version of the released Make a claim entry experience" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>06 · Continue on mobile</b><span>The same hierarchy and primary action remain clear in a narrow viewport.</span></figcaption></figure></li></ol><p class="artifact-note">Frames were selected from the full screenshot audit and de-identified for portfolio use. Select any frame to inspect it at full size.</p></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Design decisions</p><h2>Three decisions shaped the experience</h2><div class="decisions"><article><span>01</span><h3>Turn a dense form into a guided journey</h3><p>The flow focused members on one group of related information at a time, replacing long forms with a step-by-step structure.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Explain documents at the point of need</h3><p>Receipt and supporting-document guidance appeared near the relevant action, with upload instructions refined through testing.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Prevent errors before submission</h3><p>Real-time validation, clear required fields, focus behavior, and confirmation states helped members resolve issues earlier.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container grid responsive"><div><p class="eyebrow">Responsive experience</p><h2>Designed for the moments when members actually file claims</h2><p>Members may submit after a healthcare visit, from home or on a phone with receipts nearby. Reviewing both viewport families exposed more than layout differences: controls had to remain reachable, headings had to wrap predictably, and long upload and privacy content needed a usable reading order.</p><p>The released experience preserves the same information hierarchy and direct path into the claim journey across screen sizes.</p><a class="text-link" href={figmaUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore the interactive Figma prototype <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><img src="/images/medical-claims/released-flow/06-mobile-entry.webp" alt="Released Canada Life Make a claim entry page in a mobile viewport" loading="lazy"/></div></section>

<section class="prototype"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Shipped product</p><h2>See the released experience in motion</h2><p>Static screens make the interface scannable; these recordings show the sequence and responsive behavior of the product that shipped.</p></div><div class="videos"><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/wBS1dzmDpI0" title="Shipped medical claim product, web view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Shipped product · Web view</h3></article><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/KUC6sUW_B6c" title="Shipped medical claim product, mobile view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Shipped product · Mobile view</h3></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Experience model</p><h2>A clearer path from preparation to confirmation</h2><ol class="flow"><li><b>1</b><strong>Prepare</strong><small>Understand required information</small></li><li><b>2</b><strong>Describe</strong><small>Enter provider and expense details</small></li><li><b>3</b><strong>Document</strong><small>Add receipts and supporting files</small></li><li><b>4</b><strong>Review</strong><small>Confirm information</small></li><li><b>5</b><strong>Submit</strong><small>Receive next steps</small></li></ol></div></section>

  <section class="impact"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Outcome</p><h2>Validated for speed, clarity, and accessibility</h2></div><div class="impact-grid"><article><strong>30%</strong><p>faster average completion in usability testing</p></article><article><strong>WCAG AA</strong><p>accessibility level reported at launch</p></article><article><strong>Released</strong><p>to production in the group-insurance portal</p></article></div><p class="caveat">The completion-time result reflects usability testing rather than platform-wide production analytics. The project material reports fewer support calls directionally, but no verified count or measurement period was available, so that claim is not presented here as a measured result.</p></div></section>

  <section class="dark"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Accessibility and delivery</p><h2>Designed through implementation—not only handoff</h2></div><div><p>I collaborated with engineering through design reviews and worked with accessibility, QA, content, and product partners as the experience moved toward release.</p><p>The work addressed clear labels, understandable validation, contrast, keyboard and focus behavior, readable hierarchy, and mobile-friendly spacing. Continuous WCAG fixes and usability refinements continued beyond the initial launch.</p></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Reflection</p><h2>Good enterprise design makes complexity manageable</h2><p class="large">Simplifying an experience does not mean removing the system’s complexity. It means deciding when users encounter it, explaining it clearly, and working with technical partners so the intended experience survives implementation.</p><a class="button" href="/#work">Return to selected work</a></div></section>

